  back on her feet breathless no longer  laughing harry dodged behind the golden  fountain again her counter-spell hit the  head of the handsome wizard which was  blown off and landed 20 feet away  gouging long scratches into the wooden  floor  never used an unforgivable curse before  have you boy she yelled she'd abandoned  her baby voice now  you need to mean them potter you need to  really want to cause pain to enjoy it  righteous anger won't hurt me for long  i'll show you how it is done shall i  i'll give you a lesson harry was edging  around the fountain on the other side  when she screamed crucio and he was  forced to duck down again as the  centaur's arm holding its bow  spun off and landed with a crash on the  floor a short distance from the golden  wizard's head as you can see from the  passage above when harry tried to use it  on bellatrix it just didn't work that  well and the reason simply boils down to  the fact that he just wasn't sinister  enough to use it effectively even with  the fresh death of sirius harry did not  have enough hate in his heart to torture  bellatrix which is why i think it's  quite disturbing that not long after  harry was able to use the spell to its  full extent on death eater amicus caro  it's not a case of what you'll permit  minerva mcgonagall your time's over it's  us what's in charge here now and you'll  back me up before you'll pay the price  and he spat in her face harry pulled the  cloak off himself raised his wand and  said you shouldn't have done that as  amicus spun around harry shouted crucio  the death eater was lifted off his feet  he writhed through the air like a  drowning man thrashing and howling in  pain  and then with the crunch and the  shattering of glass he smashed into the  front of a bookcase and crumpled  insensible to the floor i see what  bellatrix meant said harry the blood  thundering through his brain you need to  really mean it  this passage always confused me because  while spitting in mcgonagall's face is  certainly grounds for some kind of  reaction from harry the torture curse  always seemed a bit extreme furthermore  how come harry was unable to wield it  after something incredibly dire like the  death of sirius but completely able  after something comparably innocent  expelliarmus would have been completely  appropriate in this moment but harry  chose to harness his hate and torture  someone so what gives how did this  happen was it because harry was a more  experienced wizard or is it because his  difficult life led to his ability to  harness hatred does this mean that harry  is not as good of a person as we may  have previously thought is this mostly  glossed over altercation actually one of  the darkest moments in the series there  are two possible explanations here one  harry genuinely did have hatred in his  heart and he was just embracing it  after all that harry had endured over  the years he simply became jaded and by  effectively using the torture curse he  was tapping into his new darker side  this would suggest that harry is maybe  not as pure as we initially thought  or two there is a distinction between  enjoying it and meaning it we're taught  by bellatrix who's not the most reliable  source the casting crucio requires you  to both mean it and enjoy it but i think  that the second requirement stated by  bellatrix is more of a romanticism than  anything she was inexplicably twisted  after all  i think the curse simply requires intent  meaning and in this instance harry was  able to wield the necessary intent to  cast the curse while the fact remains  that torturing someone is awful and  while harry could have just used another  spell in this particular predicament i  don't think that harry's use of the  spell necessarily means that harry  enjoyed it he simply saw a purpose for  it and wielded pure unadulterated intent  this does bring up another question  though why wasn't harry able to  effectively use the spell years earlier  harry knew what crucio was when he cast  it on bellatrix the first time so he  must have known that it required intent  why didn't it work that time was it  simply because he was a younger less  experienced wizard i think that this  boils down to a few things one harry  actually was less experienced the first  time harry tried to use the spell was  well the first time he tried to use the  spell becoming good at something  requires repetition and magic is not  excluded from this principle  why do you think harry is such an expert  at expelliarmus  two a lot going on sirius black harry's  godfather that he'd only just recently  rekindled his relationship with was just  killed by bellatrix harry still young  and having only witnessed death on a few  occasions was likely overcome with  emotion  sadness shock disgust anger these are  likely just a few of the emotions that  harry was overcome by  so it stands to reason that harry's  ability to focus in on just one of these  emotions effectively anger hatred would  have been inhibited his emotions were  simply all over the place so i'm not  surprised that he wasn't able to torture  someone in the case of mcgonagall it was  a much more controlled environment he  knew who caro was there was no real  pressure for him to act there and then  and carrow spitting in mcgonagall's face  was able to elicit a feeling of pure  rage in harry which he was able to  harness mcgonagall being spat on didn't  make him sad it made him angry which  made crucio the perfect weapon in this  moment  what do you guys think was harry's  ability to use crucio the result of pure  unadulterated and most importantly  harnessed rage did it harry enjoy  torturing karo does this scene  demonstrate a distinct lack of innocence  in harry's character leave a comment  down below with what you think and  that's it for this video if you enjoy  the content please like the video and  subscribe to the channel  until next time remember happiness can  be found even in the darkest of times if  one only remembers to turn on the light  you

